hi I'm Matt Welch editor-in-chief of  Reason magazine and I'm joined here  today by Richard fetter he's an  economics professor at Ohio University  in Ohio and author of several books  including going broke by degree why  college costs too much and you have a  serve an interesting counterintuitive  take on higher education policy in this  country that if I'm not mistaken the  government in trying to make it  affordable has made it too expensive can  you explain how that works yeah it takes  a larger percentage of a family's income  to go to college today than it did 30 40  50 years ago for almost all Americans  now that's not true of most things that  we buy it's not true of airline travel  it's not true a lot of buying  automobiles with economic growth things  usually get less burdensome with the  passage of time but with higher ed it's  the opposite and part of the reason a  huge part of the reason is because  governmental policies while a  well-intended no doubt have had sort of  an effect of increasing the demand for  higher education the number of kids  wanting to go to college without having  the same impact on the supply of higher  education meaning the prices have been  pushed up so tuition costs have gone up  at twice the rate of inflation for 34  years going up faster than medical cost  why wouldn't the law of supply and  demand suggest that people would start  universities because it's profitable  well that is exactly what is starting to  happen but the main obstacle of that  have been accreditation rules various  governmental restrictions governments  refusing to provide the financial aid  that they provide for kids to go to  government schools to go to particularly  the new proprietary or for-profit  institutions which I think have a great  deal of promise and are growing rather  fast in spite of all of these obstacles  and that's where maybe the future lies  but right now we  government drops money out of airplanes  over campuses all those campuses use  that money and efficiently they're mean  evilly organized they're not disciplined  by market forces unlike the rest of the  economy or most of the rest of the  economy and as a consequence consequence  just like medical care costs are rising  and rising and rising should states or  governments have as a policy goal to  increase the number of college graduates  I think that's a questionable objective  it's a lot about sounding objective but  I think we have to ask ourselves what  are the benefits of a college education  what are the costs of a college  education is the piece of paper we give  people called a diploma really necessary  for a lot of the work that goes on in  the workforce today 12% of the mail  carriers in the United States today now  have bachelor's degrees do you really  need a bachelor's degree to deliver the  mail in 1970 only 3% it is that suddenly  mail delivery has become much more  intellectually challenging than it did  40 years ago I don't think so  we're having more and more people who  are getting college degrees that are  having trouble getting jobs that are  really related to the intellectual  development such as it is that they get  while they're in universities so I'm  wondering whether we maybe have started  to over invest in higher education it's  a view by the way held by some other  scholars charles Murray being most  notable among them that that's many  people going to college don't have the  cognitive skills for college they're ill  prepared for college and if they do get  a degree it's often watered down and  we're just spending a lot of money to  give a person a piece of paper the obama  administration has proposed making Pell  grants something akin to an entitlement  what's your reaction about the wisdom of  that I I think it's highly questionable  first of all why should philosophically  I wonder why should government  be paying for higher education the only  justifications for government paying for  higher education is one yes  equal access we want to help people move  up the economic ladder that's the  American you gala Terry and ideal that's  an argument the other argument is that  education somehow these spillover  effects that are positive that if one  person goes to college that that tends  to promote economic growth and all all  of my research shows that these are  myths actually the more we spend on cut  more the state's spend on colleges and  universities the lure of the rate of  economic growth they have not the higher  the lower the rate of economic growth  they have and as far as equal  opportunity we have actually a smaller  pet percentage of low income people in  the college population today than we did  thirty five years ago before Pell grants  and before most of these federal  programs so they haven't worked the way  they were supposed to and some of the  notion that somehow we ought to expand  them when they haven't really been very  effective in the first place  strikes me as bizarre put off that's the  course putting in society issues like  the huge federal deficits and the  irresponsibility of the federal  government borrowing huge sums of money  and all these other macroeconomic issues  is putting those aside how and when did  you arrive at your interest in free  markets and limited government  principles and libertarianism well I'm  an economic historian by training and I  studied economic history and I did not  come at things from a libertarian  perspective or even conservative  perspective fact I have some fairly  progressive left-wing credentials in my  family uh  but the more I looked into things the  more I saw that countries with free  markets relatively stable money supplies  limited government tended to be more  prosperous grow more people mark I did a  lot of work out migration where did  people who did they move from West  Germany to East Germany or did they move  from East Germany into Western  what site when people try to cross the  Berlin Wall and get shot at that wasn't  going from west to east it was going  from east to west the West had something  resembling you know a market economy the  East had something that was quite  different  I found that markets worked and I must  do some empirical work the more  regressions I ran the more I found that  people that responded to market forces  and market forces had a positive effect  on society then of course I you know  start reading people like Rothbard and  many other thinkers who said much the  same thing and that probably reinforced  my thinking on the matter looking at the  current climate what do you see is sort  of the biggest fear that you have on the  horizon in terms of public policy or the  economy and then also what's your  biggest hope for things getting better  well I think we are in a crossroads as a  nation we as bob Pyxis wrote in his book  crisis of lieth on we have gone through  a sort of certain critical periods in  history where there's been a big upsurge  in government activity that seems to not  reverse itself we had that during the  Great Depression  someone's and we had it again in the  1960s to some extent seems to me we're  starting the possibility going through  another wave of that with the Obama  administration we are we have government  is controlled by people who are  fundamentally distrustful of capitalism  in some cases downright hostile to it  they're socialists and I don't mean that  as a pejorative term is just a fact and  I that's very concerning will they  prevail or not will they be able to  adopt the agenda i I don't however  having said that getting to the last  half-year question I have some hope  because first of all I teach 20 21 22  year-old kids and they are less  spittin with the socialist impulse the  government impulse are they they are  more open to  the possibilities that markets have  power and can do good for society then  perhaps many people realize and I really  think in the long run the Obama  experiment will prove unsuccessful and  there will be a counter-revolution if  you like the the current political  environment on the right is is  unfortunately pretty murky and without  leadership but in time that will change  and so I think long run perhaps we are  not so bad on that a hopeful note will  end there thank you very much this is  Matt Welch for recent TV
 

